"use strict";(self.webpackChunkwoocommerce_restaurant_kitchen_pos_doc=self.webpackChunkwoocommerce_restaurant_kitchen_pos_doc||[]).push([[242],{9318(e,t,n){n.r(t),n.d(t,{_pageData:()=>a,default:()=>o});var s=n(641);const a=JSON.parse('{"path":"/kitchen-customer-screen/kitchen-users.html","title":"Kitchen Users","lang":"en-US","frontmatter":{"title":"Kitchen Users","description":"Create the Kitchen User account the kitchen display signs in with, assign it to an outlet, and understand what the role can and cannot reach."},"git":{},"filePathRelative":"kitchen-customer-screen/kitchen-users.md"}'),r={name:"kitchen-users.md"},o=(0,n(6262).A)(r,[["render",function(e,t,n,a,r,o){const h=(0,s.g2)("RouteLink");return(0,s.uX)(),(0,s.CE)("div",null,[t[3]||(t[3]=(0,s.Fv)('<h1 id="kitchen-users" tabindex="-1"><a class="header-anchor" href="#kitchen-users"><span>Kitchen Users</span></a></h1><p>The kitchen display is not open to the public. Somebody has to sign in, and only an account holding the <strong>Kitchen User</strong> role is let through.</p><p>That role is created for you when the plugin is activated. You do not have to add it by hand.</p><h2 id="what-a-kitchen-user-can-do" tabindex="-1"><a class="header-anchor" href="#what-a-kitchen-user-can-do"><span>What a Kitchen User can do</span></a></h2><table><thead><tr><th>Can</th><th>Cannot</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Sign in at the kitchen address and work the order board</td><td>Open the WordPress admin</td></tr><tr><td>Move tickets between On Hold, Preparing, Completed and Sent</td><td>Use the Point of Sale till</td></tr><tr><td>Read the kitchen note on a ticket</td><td>See or change any setting</td></tr><tr><td>Switch the board between light and dark, and go full screen</td><td>See another outlet&#39;s tickets</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The role is deliberately narrow. It is safe to hand out to kitchen staff on a shared tablet.</p><h2 id="create-a-kitchen-user" tabindex="-1"><a class="header-anchor" href="#create-a-kitchen-user"><span>Create a Kitchen User</span></a></h2><ol><li>Go to <strong>Restaurant POS → Users</strong> and select <strong>Add New</strong>.</li><li>Fill in the account details as you would for any Point of Sale user — username, email, password and the <strong>Outlet</strong> the person works at.</li><li>In the <strong>User Role</strong> field, choose <strong>Kitchen User</strong>.</li><li>Save.</li></ol><p><img src="/kitchen-and-customer-screen-for-restaurant-pos/images/kitchen-customer-screen/users-add-kitchen-user.png" alt="The User Role field on the Point of Sale Add User form"></p><p>The <strong>User Role</strong> field offers two choices:</p><table><thead><tr><th>Choice</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Pos User</strong></td><td>An ordinary till operator. This is the default.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Kitchen User</strong></td><td>An account that can only reach the kitchen display.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class="hint-container important"><p class="hint-container-title">Important</p><p>The <strong>Outlet</strong> you choose matters. The kitchen board shows the tickets of the outlet the signed in user is mapped to. A Kitchen User with no outlet sees an empty board even when the kitchen is busy.</p></div><h2 id="review-the-accounts-you-have" tabindex="-1"><a class="header-anchor" href="#review-the-accounts-you-have"><span>Review the accounts you have</span></a></h2><p><strong>Restaurant POS → Users</strong> lists both kinds of account together, with a <strong>User Role</strong> column added by this module so you can tell them apart at a glance.</p><p><img src="/kitchen-and-customer-screen-for-restaurant-pos/images/kitchen-customer-screen/users-pos-user-list.png" alt="The Point of Sale user list with the User Role column"></p><h2 id="change-an-existing-account-s-role" tabindex="-1"><a class="header-anchor" href="#change-an-existing-account-s-role"><span>Change an existing account&#39;s role</span></a></h2><p>Open the account from <strong>Restaurant POS → Users</strong>, change <strong>User Role</strong> and save. The change applies immediately; the person is signed out of the screen they no longer have access to the next time they load it.</p><h2 id="delete-a-kitchen-user" tabindex="-1"><a class="header-anchor" href="#delete-a-kitchen-user"><span>Delete a Kitchen User</span></a></h2><p>Use the delete action on the Point of Sale user list. Deleting a Kitchen User removes the WordPress account and clears that person from the outlet mapping, and the admin confirms with <em>&quot;User deleted successfully.&quot;</em> If you try the same action on an account that is neither a POS User nor a Kitchen User, the admin answers <em>&quot;Not a valid pos user.&quot;</em> and nothing is deleted.</p><h2 id="signing-in" tabindex="-1"><a class="header-anchor" href="#signing-in"><span>Signing in</span></a></h2><p>Send the kitchen staff to your kitchen address, for example <code>https://yourstore.com/kitchen</code>. They see a sign in card, enter the username and password, and land straight on the board.</p><p><img src="/kitchen-and-customer-screen-for-restaurant-pos/images/kitchen-customer-screen/kitchen-login.png" alt="The kitchen sign in screen"></p><p>Two messages can appear on that card:</p><ul><li><strong>Error : Invalid username and password.</strong> — the username or password is wrong.</li><li><strong>You are not allowed to access this page.</strong> — the person is signed in, but with an account that does not hold the Kitchen User role. Sign out and sign in with a Kitchen User account.</li></ul><h2 id="next-step" tabindex="-1"><a class="header-anchor" href="#next-step"><span>Next step</span></a></h2>',25)),(0,s.Lk)("p",null,[t[1]||(t[1]=(0,s.eW)("Continue with ",-1)),(0,s.bF)(h,{to:"/kitchen-customer-screen/customer-screen-setup.html"},{default:(0,s.k6)(()=>[...t[0]||(t[0]=[(0,s.eW)("Customer Screen Setup",-1)])]),_:1}),t[2]||(t[2]=(0,s.eW)(".",-1))])])}]])}}]);